<!--
  VulnTrade - base anchors (ids 110090-110099).

  Pipeline: Log4j2 JSON -> Fluent Bit -> S3 -> SQS -> aws-s3 wodle.
  The wodle wraps each record as {"integration":"aws","aws":{...record...}},
  so the stock json decoder exposes every top-level field as aws.<field>
  (aws.eventType, aws.clientIp) and every details key as aws.details.<key>
  (aws.details.reason). Every such event lands on stock rule 80200 (AWS,
  level 0), which is our anchor. All scalars arrive as strings: "7", "true",
  "1E+7".

  Files load alphabetically from etc/rules/, hence the numeric prefixes.

  RULE CONVENTIONS (all 7 files follow these; also in docs/logging-guide.md 6.2)
   1. Every <field> and <regex> uses type="pcre2", with (?i) where case does
      not matter (roles, booleans, user agents, SQL keywords, amounts).
   2. One base rule per eventType, directly under 110091. Refinements
      (reason, path, isOwner, amount, action) are children of that base rule.
   3. Bursts (frequency + timeframe) count the base rule. Extra conditions go
      on a child of the burst. Wazuh remembers an event only under the rule it
      finally lands on, so an event that lands on a refinement child is not
      counted by a burst on the base rule.
      If the burst must count those children too, tag the base rule and the
      children with one vt_* group and count the group:
      <if_sid>base</if_sid> + <if_matched_group>vt_group</if_matched_group>
      (the login bursts in vulntrade-10-auth.xml). Never also point an
      if_matched_sid at a rule in that group: Wazuh then keeps the rule's
      events only for the if_matched_sid, and the group count misses them.
   4. Never use level 0 below 110090 (level-0 events are not remembered).
      Level 2 = remembered for correlation, no alert.
   5. Neutral-name events (password_changed, api_key_used, refresh_token_used,
      sensitive_data_read, data_exported, ...) carry their result only in
      aws.outcome. A rule that means one result filters on aws.outcome.
   6. Correlation keys: aws.requestId (two facts from one HTTP request),
      aws.sessionId (STOMP), aws.clientIp / aws.username /
      aws.details.attemptedUsername (bursts).
   7. A rule may reference only ids in its own file or an earlier file, and a
      parent rule must appear above its children.
   8. Booleans match (?i)^(true|false)$. Amounts may look like 1E+7 or 1e+20,
      so amount patterns accept e or E and an optional +.

  Descriptions start with "VulnTrade: ". A detection from the team sheet puts
  its sheet name right after, e.g. "VulnTrade: brute_force_detected - ...".

  Rule order: among the children of one rule, Wazuh tries the higher level
  first. That is why a Log4Shell string (110100, level 12) beats the event's
  own eventType rule (110091, level 3), and why 110140 is level 13.
-->
<group name="vulntrade,">

  <!-- Any VulnTrade line, either file. Level 0: a silent anchor for the
       content rules in vulntrade-20-injection.xml. -->
  <rule id="110090" level="0">
    <if_sid>80200</if_sid>
    <field name="aws.source_file" type="pcre2">^/var/log/vulntrade/</field>
    <description>VulnTrade: application log record</description>
  </rule>

  <!-- Any security.log event. Accepts any \w+ name, so it works whichever
       side (app or rules) deploys first; the naming rule (lower snake case)
       lives in the Java SecurityEvent enum. Events with no rule of their own
       (session_created, price_alert_created, ...) stop here. -->
  <rule id="110091" level="3">
    <if_sid>110090</if_sid>
    <field name="aws.source_file" type="pcre2">^/var/log/vulntrade/security\.log$</field>
    <field name="aws.eventType" type="pcre2">^\w+$</field>
    <description>VulnTrade: security event $(aws.eventType) ($(aws.outcome))</description>
    <group>vulntrade_security,</group>
  </rule>

</group>
